Z||000002||qwikgta||06-07-2001||11:10 PM||||Thanks 88.<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar.<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||06-08-2001||01:35 AM||||Yeah you will HAVE to cut them to the original length or use the old "sleeves".  There is no way you are fitting the ones the Fiero Store gives you in there.||||reg||1
Z||000004||00lE||06-08-2001||02:42 AM||||I am amazed that you would actually have to cut a brand new part advertised to fit an 88 Fiero by a company that supposedly "specializes" in Fiero parts.  What kind of business practice is that??||||reg||1
Z||000005||Coop88||06-08-2001||06:35 AM||||I got my endlinks from suspension.com. I didn't need to cut anything. The new sleeves are just a little shorter than the originals. Not by much though. The originals measured about 2 1/4". Worked out pretty good.<p>[This message has been edited by Coop88 (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||06-08-2001||08:27 AM||||I bought front and rear bar endlinks from PepBoys, were $15/pair. Or, you can buy the same from AutoZone for $10/pair.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||06-08-2001||09:32 AM||||'Tis my understanding that the length of the end links should be set so that the sway bar arms are parallel to the ground when the vehicle is setting on the suspension. Don't forget to get your brackets.||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||06-08-2001||08:11 PM||||My rear anti-sway bar end links measure 2-1/4 inches on my 88 Formula. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Toddster||06-08-2001||08:28 PM||||you need (1) 2-1/4 inch sleeve, (4) washers, (1) 5/16 x 7 inch bolt, (4) bushings (poly is recommended, and (1) 5/16 inch nut...for each side<P>good luck||||reg||1
